              "submitted_date": null,
              "patch_version": 1,
              "series_patch_count": null,
              "version_history": [],
              "series_items": [],
              "patch_summary": "This patch refactors the reclaim logic from try_charge_memcg() into two new helper functions: memcg_charge_may_reclaim() and memcg_charge_reclaim(). The former checks if a memory charge is allowed, while the latter performs the actual reclamation. This separation allows for easier reuse of the reclaim logic in subsequent patches.",
              "analysis_source": "llm-chunked-no-replies",
              "review_comments": [],
              "list_id": "cgroups@vger.kernel.org"
            },
            {
              "activity_type": "patch_submitted",
              "subject": "[PATCH v1 2/3] mm: memcontrol: add mem_cgroup_reclaim_for_batch()",
              "message_id": "20261003001555.3498357-3-joannelkoong@gmail.com",
              "url": "https://lore.kernel.org/all/20261003001555.3498357-3-joannelkoong@gmail.com/",
              "date": "2026-10-03T00:19:06Z",
              "in_reply_to": null,
              "ack_type": null,
              "is_ongoing": false,
              "submitted_date": null,
              "patch_version": 1,
              "series_patch_count": null,
              "version_history": [],
              "series_items": [],
              "patch_summary": "This patch adds a new function, mem_cgroup_reclaim_for_batch(), which allows callers to reclaim memory from a memory cgroup in batches instead of one folio at a time. This is useful for callers that charge a batch of folios one at a time, such as readahead, and can trigger multiple reclaim passes when the memory cgroup limit is reached. The function retries reclaim only once if it fails, making it suitable for callers that are okay with charges failing. The patch also adds a new function to internal.h to provide a default implementation for non-MEMCG configurations.",
              "analysis_source": "llm-chunked-no-replies",
              "review_comments": [],
              "list_id": "cgroups@vger.kernel.org"
            },
            {
              "activity_type": "patch_submitted",
              "subject": "[PATCH v1 3/3] mm/readahead: avoid per-folio memcg reclaim",
              "message_id": "20261003001555.3498357-4-joannelkoong@gmail.com",
              "url": "https://lore.kernel.org/all/20261003001555.3498357-4-joannelkoong@gmail.com/",
              "date": "2026-10-03T00:19:10Z",
              "in_reply_to": null,
              "ack_type": null,
              "is_ongoing": false,
              "submitted_date": null,
              "patch_version": 1,
              "series_patch_count": null,
              "version_history": [],
              "series_items": [],
              "patch_summary": "This patch modifies the readahead mechanism to avoid per-folio memory cgroup (memcg) reclaim, which was causing livelocks and high CPU usage on systems with many tasks faulting in the same cgroup. Instead of charging each folio separately, the patch adds multiple folios to the page cache at once without direct reclaim, and only reclaims for the rest of the window when the memcg is at its limit. If the memcg hits its limit again before adding all folios, it attempts one more time. If there's still no room, readahead stops early rather than trying harder. This change affects only speculative readahead folios and does not alter behavior for actual fault or read requests.",
              "analysis_source": "llm-chunked-no-replies",
              "review_comments": [],
              "list_id": "cgroups@vger.kernel.org"
            }
          ],
          "patch_summary": "This patch series addresses a performance issue in Linux's readahead feature by avoiding per-folio memory cgroup (memcg) reclaim. When multiple tasks fault in the same cgroup, the frequent per-folio charges trigger reclaim, leading to livelocks and high CPU usage. The patches modify readahead to charge all folios in its window at once when the memcg is at its limit, reducing reclaim passes by 94-96% and eliminating livelocks on a test system with btrfs file systems and compress-force=zstd:3.",
          "analysis_source": "llm-chunked-no-replies",
          "review_comments": [],
          "list_id": "cgroups@vger.kernel.org"
        }
